{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Secrets & Credentials Security Review",
  "description": "Recurring security review that scans the workspace repos (json-server, gson, click) for exposed secrets and credentials. INTENDED CADENCE: HOURLY. There is no native cron/time trigger type for Kiro hooks, so the true hourly schedule is driven by the companion cron entry documented in scripts/security/README.md (crontab line: `0 * * * *`). This hook is the in-Kiro entry point to the exact same review and can also be run manually (userTriggered) at any time. See scripts/security/README.md for scheduling details and the weekly-vs-hourly note.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "Perform the recurring secrets/credentials security review.\n\n1. Run the companion scan script from the json-server repo root: `bash scripts/security/secret-scan.sh`. This script scans all three sibling repos in the workspace (json-server, gson, click) by resolving the workspace root as the parent of json-server (overridable via the SCAN_ROOT env var). It prefers gitleaks when installed and falls back to a built-in regex scan otherwise.\n\n2. Read the most recent generated findings report under `json-server/.security/reports/` (files named `secret-scan-<UTC-timestamp>.md` and `.json`). Use the newest timestamp.\n\n3. Summarize the findings grouped by severity: CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW. For each finding, explicitly flag the exposed secret or credential with its repository, file path, and line number. Do NOT print the full secret value back; keep it redacted.\n\n4. State the script exit code contract outcome: exit 0 means clean, exit 2 means secrets were found. If secrets were found, call them out prominently and recommend immediate rotation of the affected credentials.\n\nKeep this review self-contained so it works whether triggered on the documented hourly cron cadence or manually inside Kiro."
  }
}
